OK, so I'm going to be closing all of those down now.

68
00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:57.550
And let's go ahead and start building our material

69
00:02:57.550 --> 00:02:58.949
from scratch.

70
00:02:58.949 --> 00:03:01.990
So I'm going to navigate to the in class material folder.

71
00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:04.669
And then under Dissolve in Class,

72
00:03:04.669 --> 00:03:07.389
I'm going to be creating our parent material

73
00:03:07.389 --> 00:03:11.979
that I'm going to be calling M underscore Dissolve.

74
00:03:11.979 --> 00:03:14.419
And then I'm going to be creating a material function

75
00:03:14.419 --> 00:03:16.340
by right clicking in the content browser

76
00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:19.740
and then navigating to Material, Advanced,

77
00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:21.099
and Material Function.

78
00:03:21.139 --> 00:03:22.939
The main naming convention for this one

79
00:03:22.939 --> 00:03:26.020
is MF underscore, and then I'm going

80
00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:28.689
to be calling it teleporter.

81
00:03:28.689 --> 00:03:32.469
Then I'll Control-Shift-S to save all my changes.

82
00:03:32.469 --> 00:03:34.750
All right, let's go ahead and open up

83
00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:36.629
our material function.

84
00:03:36.629 --> 00:03:39.669
All right, so first of all, if we check out

85
00:03:39.669 --> 00:03:41.349
the stats panel, we can see that we're

86
00:03:41.349 --> 00:03:43.310
getting an arrow because we're missing

87
00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:45.550
the function output result.

88
00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:48.050
And that's really just because we're previewing this node

89
00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:50.590
already, even though nothing is connected to it.

90
00:03:50.629 --> 00:03:53.550
So if we right click on it and stop previewing the node,

91
00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:56.240
that arrow will be gone.

92
00:03:56.240 --> 00:03:57.919
Now, before we get started, I just

93
00:03:57.919 --> 00:04:00.080
wanted to mention that we've talked in depth

94
00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:03.759
about material functions in the Masking Materials and Material

95
00:04:03.759 --> 00:04:05.280
Functions course already.

96
00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:06.919
So if you haven't checked that out yet,

97
00:04:06.919 --> 00:04:09.199
I'd encourage you to watch that series of classes

98
00:04:09.199 --> 00:04:13.560
first to better understand what we'll be developing today.

99
00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:15.360
The development of material functions

100
00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:17.120
can be quite abstract if you're just

101
00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:19.439
following along without having a rough idea

102
00:04:19.439 --> 00:04:20.639
of the final picture.

103
00:04:20.639 --> 00:04:23.319
And we won't be able to preview what we're creating today

104
00:04:23.319 --> 00:04:25.620
until we really embed this material function

105
00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:28.399
into its parent material and play around with its parameters

106
00:04:28.399 --> 00:04:30.319
in a material instance.

107
00:04:30.319 --> 00:04:32.300
So I'll be overlaying some visual previews

108
00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:35.040
of the final product as we draft this out.

109
00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.899
And this should help you better understand what we're doing.

110
00:04:37.899 --> 00:04:40.040
All right, let's start out by panning a little bit

111
00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:42.839
to the left because this material is

112
00:04:42.839 --> 00:04:47.279
going to be quite extensive and will need a lot of space.

113
00:04:47.279 --> 00:04:49.579
OK, then in an empty area of the graph,

114
00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:55.379
I'm going to be right clicking and search for a bounding box

115
00:04:55.379 --> 00:04:58.220
based 0 to 1 UV.

116
00:04:58.220 --> 00:05:00.860
So this bounding box material function

117
00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:02.779
is actually a material function

118
00:05:02.779 --> 00:05:06.379
that generates normalized UV coordinates in the 0 to 1

119
00:05:06.379 --> 00:05:09.660
range based on an object bounding box.

120
00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:12.620
A bounding box is an invisible rectangular box

121
00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:17.250
that perfectly fits around your entire 3D object.

122
00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:19.930
And since it is a material function, as I just mentioned,

123
00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:22.009
if you double click on it, you will

124
00:05:22.009 --> 00:05:25.449
be able to actually see how it was developed.

125
00:05:25.449 --> 00:05:28.410
But as usual, make sure to not apply any changes

126
00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:31.850
if you move any nodes around because this material function

127
00:05:31.850 --> 00:05:33.930
is part of our edging content.

128
00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:36.180
So I'm just going to close it down.

129
00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:38.339
And the first we want to do is actually

130
00:05:38.339 --> 00:05:42.819
invert this 0 to 1 range with a 1 minus node.

131
00:05:42.819 --> 00:05:45.699
So I'm going to drag out of the RGB output pin

132
00:05:45.699 --> 00:05:48.430
and then search for 1 minus.

133
00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:54.350
OK, now we want to go on and create a function input.

134
00:05:54.350 --> 00:05:56.709
Function input represent the exposed pins

135
00:05:56.709 --> 00:05:58.829
when a material function gets embedded

136
00:05:58.829 --> 00:06:00.589
into its parent material.

137
00:06:00.589 --> 00:06:02.389
They kind of work like parameters

138
00:06:02.389 --> 00:06:04.310
for material instances.

139
00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:06.069
In this first section of the graph,

140
00:06:06.069 --> 00:06:08.629
we will be exposing a few pins to be able to control

141
00:06:08.629 --> 00:06:11.910
the way we cut our material.

142
00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:14.269
And this vector tree constant in particular

143
00:06:14.269 --> 00:06:17.790
will allow us to define the shape and overall extension

144
00:06:17.790 --> 00:06:23.670
of the cut in the x, y, and z vertex position of our object.

145
00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:28.629
Because as usual, the RGB values of a vector tree node

146
00:06:28.629 --> 00:06:32.110
represent the x, y, and z-coordinates in the world.

147
00:06:32.110 --> 00:06:38.149
So a full red would mean a full cut in the x-axis.

148
00:06:38.149 --> 00:06:42.149
A full green would mean a full cut in the y-axis.

149
00:06:42.149 --> 00:06:46.029
And a full blue, a full cut in the z-axis.

150
00:06:46.069 --> 00:06:48.870
For now, we're going to keep this value as black.

151
00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:53.819
So let's name this function input plane cut.

152
00:06:53.819 --> 00:06:57.500
And make sure that use preview value as default is turned on.

153
00:06:57.500 --> 00:06:59.980
We're going to leave the sort priority at 0.

154
00:06:59.980 --> 00:07:02.660
OK, now after this 1 minus node,

155
00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:04.660
we want to be adding a dot product.

156
00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:07.300
So I'm going to extend out of the output pin

157
00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:10.939
and then type dot to create a dot product.

158
00:07:10.939 --> 00:07:12.699
And then we want to connect the output

159
00:07:12.699 --> 00:07:16.100
pin of the plane cut to the b input of the dot.

160
00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:19.620
Now the dot product computes the length of one vector

161
00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:21.699
projected onto another.

162
00:07:21.699 --> 00:07:23.459
We've also used it in the development

163
00:07:23.459 --> 00:07:28.379
of cotton and silk-like shaders in lesson 3 for faking effects.

164
00:07:28.379 --> 00:07:30.899
Now since we're using the bounding box material function,

165
00:07:30.899 --> 00:07:32.540
the result of this dot product

166
00:07:32.540 --> 00:07:35.180
will be local space or object-oriented,

167
00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:37.500
so relative to our object.

168
00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:41.060
If we were to rotate the object around in the 3D space,

169
00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:44.300
the cut will stay as it is, as you can see.

170
00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:47.660
Now we want to add more granular control over our cut.

171
00:07:47.660 --> 00:07:50.100
And we want to define where exactly it will

172
00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:52.180
take place on our object.

173
00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:55.060
So let's add one more function input

174
00:07:55.060 --> 00:08:00.389
and call it length, then switch its input type from vector 3

175
00:08:00.389 --> 00:08:03.670
to scalar, and then make sure that use preview value

176
00:08:03.670 --> 00:08:05.910
as default is turned on.

177
00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:07.509
Now what we get from the dot product

178
00:08:07.509 --> 00:08:10.230
is basically a gradient ranging from 0

179
00:08:10.230 --> 00:08:15.430
to 1 across our chosen axis, either the x, the y, or the z.

180
00:08:15.430 --> 00:08:17.750
If we now drag out of the dot product

181
00:08:17.750 --> 00:08:20.589
and we add a divide node, we're

182
00:08:20.589 --> 00:08:23.029
basically scaling down the gradient range

183
00:08:23.029 --> 00:08:24.790
that we've got from the dot product

184
00:08:24.790 --> 00:08:27.790
and essentially zooming into it.

185
00:08:27.790 --> 00:08:30.589
So let's plug the length scalar output

186
00:08:30.589 --> 00:08:32.870
into the b input of the divide.

187
00:08:32.870 --> 00:08:36.669
And since this division is happening after our dot

188
00:08:36.669 --> 00:08:40.549
product is not going to impact the cut in its three

189
00:08:40.549 --> 00:08:44.350
dimensions, this scalar function input

190
00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:47.029
will just allow us to basically tweak

191
00:08:47.029 --> 00:08:49.549
how extended our cut will be.

192
00:08:49.549 --> 00:08:53.990
Now let's allow ourselves to add a texture on top

193
00:08:53.990 --> 00:08:56.190
of the sliced area only.

194
00:08:56.190 --> 00:08:58.789
So by pressing A on my keyboard and clicking

195
00:08:58.789 --> 00:09:02.350
on an empty area of the graph, I will be adding an add node.

196
00:09:02.350 --> 00:09:04.590
And then I will connect the output

197
00:09:04.629 --> 00:09:06.950
pin of the divide node to the a.

198
00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:12.070
And I will go down here and add yet one more function input

199
00:09:12.070 --> 00:09:15.580
that I will be calling texture.

200
00:09:15.580 --> 00:09:18.580
I will use the prevalue as default

201
00:09:18.580 --> 00:09:21.259
and then set the sort priority to 2.

202
00:09:21.259 --> 00:09:24.659
And let's make also sure that the length scalar has

203
00:09:24.659 --> 00:09:27.330
a sort priority of 1.

204
00:09:27.330 --> 00:09:32.659
OK, now let's create one more function input that's

205
00:09:32.659 --> 00:09:37.120
going to be called fake texture.

206
00:09:37.159 --> 00:09:39.789
It's going to be a scalar.

207
00:09:39.789 --> 00:09:42.029
We're going to use the prevalue as default.

208
00:09:42.029 --> 00:09:45.210
And its sorting priority is going to be 3.

209
00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:47.370
And then if I press M on the keyboard

210
00:09:47.370 --> 00:09:50.730
and click on an empty area of the graph, I can add a multiply.

211
00:09:50.730 --> 00:09:54.850
And we want to multiply the input texture with the input

212
00:09:54.850 --> 00:09:56.090
fake texture.

213
00:09:56.090 --> 00:09:59.049
Basically, by multiplying these two nodes,

214
00:09:59.049 --> 00:10:00.970
we're defining how much of the texture

215
00:10:00.970 --> 00:10:03.649
we want to see based on the sliced area

216
00:10:03.649 --> 00:10:06.100
that it is applied to.

217
00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:08.779
So let's now connect the multiply output pin

218
00:10:08.779 --> 00:10:12.049
to the B input of the add.

219
00:10:12.049 --> 00:10:15.450
OK, now let's add even more granular control here.

220
00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:18.129
I'm going to be moving the output result to the side.

221
00:10:18.129 --> 00:10:21.049
And now we want to be able to linearly dissolve

222
00:10:21.049 --> 00:10:25.370
the sliced area without distorting its extension.

223
00:10:25.370 --> 00:10:27.690
So if I extend out of the add node,

224
00:10:27.690 --> 00:10:30.169
we can add add subtract node that will

225
00:10:30.169 --> 00:10:32.580
allow us to do exactly that.

226
00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:35.539
Now let's create one more function input

227
00:10:35.539 --> 00:10:40.159
that we're going to be calling dissolve offset.

228
00:10:40.159 --> 00:10:44.720
It's going to be a scalar used for the value as default.

229
00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:48.360
And the sorting priority is going to be 4.

230
00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:50.240
And then we want to plug it to the B

231
00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:52.240
input of the subtract.

232
00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:56.279
OK, now let's compare it with the length function input

233
00:10:56.279 --> 00:10:57.879
that we exposed.

234
00:10:57.879 --> 00:11:00.919
While the length will either extend or reduce

235
00:11:00.919 --> 00:11:04.159
the overall area of our cut, the dissolve scalar

236
00:11:04.159 --> 00:11:06.919
will simply allow us to offset the sliced area

237
00:11:06.919 --> 00:11:08.600
along our subject.

238
00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:10.399
Now as we expand this node, we need

239
00:11:10.399 --> 00:11:14.360
the result of this subtract node to range from 0 to 1

240
00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:17.200
rather than larger values, especially

241
00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:20.440
because we'll be using it as an alpha mask in lerp nodes

242
00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:21.759
as we move along.

243
00:11:21.759 --> 00:11:26.440
So we want to add a clamp node after this subtract

244
00:11:26.440 --> 00:11:28.159
before proceeding further.

245
00:11:28.159 --> 00:11:30.000
And this is going to be providing us

246
00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:32.240
with more predictable results because we're

247
00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:35.379
going to be keeping the range from 0 to 1.

248
00:11:35.379 --> 00:11:39.220
So now we're able to granularly control our sliced

249
00:11:39.220 --> 00:11:42.460
area and offset it around if we want to.

250
00:11:42.460 --> 00:11:44.620
We can also add a texture to it

251
00:11:44.620 --> 00:11:47.940
and define how much of the texture we want to see.

252
00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:50.019
Now let's give us more artistic control

253
00:11:50.019 --> 00:11:53.539
by allowing us to tint the interior surface of our sliced

254
00:11:53.539 --> 00:11:55.779
area to any color we want.

255
00:11:55.779 --> 00:11:58.059
So let's add one more function input.

256
00:11:58.059 --> 00:12:00.620
I know that's a lot of function inputs.

257
00:12:00.620 --> 00:12:05.730
And we're going to be calling it interiorTint.

258
00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:08.169
The input type is going to be vector3.

259
00:12:08.169 --> 00:12:10.490
We want to use the preview value as default.

260
00:12:10.490 --> 00:12:13.620
And the sort priority is going to be 5.

261
00:12:13.620 --> 00:12:18.059
Now we want to be adding the tint only to our sliced area.

262
00:12:18.059 --> 00:12:21.500
So we'll need to use the result of this clamp node

263
00:12:21.500 --> 00:12:24.220
as alpha mask in a lerp.

264
00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:26.460
So let's press L on the keyboard

265
00:12:26.460 --> 00:12:28.419
and click in an interior of the graph

266
00:12:28.500 --> 00:12:31.340
and then connect the clamp to the alpha,

267
00:12:31.340 --> 00:12:33.620
interiorTint to the A,

268
00:12:33.620 --> 00:12:35.899
and then I'm going to be pressing 1 on my keyboard

269
00:12:35.899 --> 00:12:38.659
and add a constant value of 0

270
00:12:38.659 --> 00:12:41.700
that we're really going to be using as a dummy value

271
00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:43.419
because we don't want the tint to be applied

272
00:12:43.419 --> 00:12:47.100
to the unsliced area of our object at all.

273
00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:49.659
So I'm just going to be plugging it to the B.

274
00:12:49.659 --> 00:12:51.820
Okay, if we see an example of how the material

275
00:12:51.820 --> 00:12:54.500
looks like now, we can see this black area

276
00:12:54.500 --> 00:12:57.059
on the interior part of our object.

277
00:12:57.100 --> 00:13:00.179
That's because our shader is not two-sided.

278
00:13:00.179 --> 00:13:03.620
So only its outer normals are being calculated.

279
00:13:03.620 --> 00:13:06.299
We need a way to tell Unreal that we want our tint

280
00:13:06.299 --> 00:13:09.220
to affect the interior side of the object.

281
00:13:09.220 --> 00:13:11.899
And we have a very useful material expression to do that

282
00:13:11.899 --> 00:13:14.139
called two-sided sign.

283
00:13:14.139 --> 00:13:16.379
So let's right-click in an emptier of the graph

284
00:13:16.379 --> 00:13:19.860
and add a two-sided sign expression.

285
00:13:19.860 --> 00:13:22.860
Now we want to use this two-sided sign information

286
00:13:22.860 --> 00:13:25.539
as the driver and alpha to define

287
00:13:25.539 --> 00:13:28.740
where we will be displaying our texture

288
00:13:28.740 --> 00:13:31.120
versus the tinted color.

289
00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:34.120
So let's add one more Lerp node by pressing L

290
00:13:34.120 --> 00:13:36.559
on the keyboard and left-clicking on the graph.

291
00:13:36.559 --> 00:13:39.080
And then we want to plug the two-sided sign

292
00:13:39.080 --> 00:13:44.070
into the alpha, the result of the Lerp to the B,

293
00:13:44.070 --> 00:13:47.190
and the interior tint to the A.

294
00:13:47.190 --> 00:13:48.870
Now the result of this Lerp node

295
00:13:48.870 --> 00:13:51.309
can be connected to the output result.

296
00:13:51.309 --> 00:13:54.029
And it will be providing us with the formation

297
00:13:54.029 --> 00:13:57.870
to cover the emissive color of our main material.

298
00:13:57.870 --> 00:14:01.070
But we need one more output pen

299
00:14:01.070 --> 00:14:04.429
to output our opacity mask for our main material

300
00:14:04.429 --> 00:14:08.149
because this material needs to have transparent areas.

301
00:14:08.149 --> 00:14:10.350
Now for now, we've always been dealing

302
00:14:10.350 --> 00:14:12.669
with function inputs to allow anybody

303
00:14:12.669 --> 00:14:14.070
using our material function

304
00:14:14.070 --> 00:14:18.509
to input information into our function.

305
00:14:18.509 --> 00:14:20.669
And now we're gonna be right-clicking on the graph

306
00:14:20.710 --> 00:14:24.549
and we're gonna be adding a function output

307
00:14:24.549 --> 00:14:28.190
to add another output pen to our material function.

308
00:14:28.190 --> 00:14:29.990
Now let's go ahead and rename

309
00:14:29.990 --> 00:14:34.679
this first function output to emissive.

310
00:14:34.679 --> 00:14:37.200
And we can keep the sort priority to zero.

311
00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:39.600
And then this second function output

312
00:14:39.600 --> 00:14:42.440
is gonna be called opacity mask.

313
00:14:42.440 --> 00:14:46.059
And the sort priority is gonna be one.

314
00:14:46.059 --> 00:14:50.419
And lastly, we wanna connect the output pen of the clamp

315
00:14:50.419 --> 00:14:53.700
to the output opacity mask, okay?

316
00:14:53.700 --> 00:14:57.139
The last thing we wanna do now before applying our changes

317
00:14:57.139 --> 00:14:59.820
is just clicking in an empty area of the graph

318
00:14:59.820 --> 00:15:04.340
and then expose our material function to our library.

319
00:15:04.340 --> 00:15:06.100
Now we can save

320
00:15:06.100 --> 00:15:08.460
and the changes will be applied automatically.

321
00:15:08.460 --> 00:15:11.299
Okay, let's click on this icon over here

322
00:15:11.299 --> 00:15:13.379
to navigate to the content browser

323
00:15:13.379 --> 00:15:18.149
and open up our parent material.

324
00:15:18.149 --> 00:15:19.309
The first thing we wanna do

325
00:15:19.309 --> 00:15:21.909
is click in an empty area of the graph

326
00:15:21.909 --> 00:15:26.070
and change the blend mode from opaque to masked

327
00:15:26.070 --> 00:15:29.129
to have access to the opacity mask.

328
00:15:29.129 --> 00:15:31.129
Then we wanna right-click on base color

329
00:15:31.129 --> 00:15:33.570
and promote these two parameter.

330
00:15:33.570 --> 00:15:36.250
And this base color is basically gonna be

331
00:15:36.250 --> 00:15:39.090
the color of our unslized area.

332
00:15:39.090 --> 00:15:40.370
So I'm gonna double-click on it

333
00:15:40.370 --> 00:15:42.250
and you can choose any color you want.

334
00:15:42.250 --> 00:15:45.539
I'm gonna choose something like orange.

335
00:15:45.539 --> 00:15:47.860
Now we can right-click in an empty area of the graph

336
00:15:47.860 --> 00:15:51.120
and then type mf teleporter

337
00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:55.460
to embed our material function into its parent material.

338
00:15:55.460 --> 00:15:57.740
And then we can right-click on plain cut

339
00:15:57.740 --> 00:16:00.409
and promote two parameter.

340
00:16:00.409 --> 00:16:02.370
And we can color these fully blue

341
00:16:02.370 --> 00:16:06.929
cause we want our cut to happen on the Z axis.

342
00:16:06.929 --> 00:16:11.169
Then let's right-click on length and promote two parameter.

343
00:16:11.169 --> 00:16:13.009
We can keep the length at zero

344
00:16:13.009 --> 00:16:16.370
but let's change the slider max value to three

345
00:16:16.370 --> 00:16:18.730
to contain the maximum length.

346
00:16:18.730 --> 00:16:21.529
Now let's right-click in an empty area of the graph

347
00:16:22.049 --> 00:16:23.570
and add a texture sample.

348
00:16:23.570 --> 00:16:25.289
And then we want to right-click on it

349
00:16:25.289 --> 00:16:27.210
and convert these two parameter.

350
00:16:27.210 --> 00:16:31.879
And we can just simply call it texture effects.

351
00:16:31.879 --> 00:16:33.799
The type of texture we wanna use

352
00:16:33.799 --> 00:16:39.200
is the widget grid vertex color material.

353
00:16:39.200 --> 00:16:42.000
And then if we press P on our keyboard

354
00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:43.840
and left-click in an empty area of the graph

355
00:16:43.840 --> 00:16:47.269
we can add a panner to animate this texture.

356
00:16:47.269 --> 00:16:51.419
And we can set the speed Y to something small like 0.2

357
00:16:51.419 --> 00:16:53.340
and then connect the output pin of the panner

358
00:16:53.340 --> 00:16:55.700
to the UVs of the texture effects

359
00:16:55.700 --> 00:16:58.919
and then the RGB of the texture effect to the texture.

360
00:16:58.919 --> 00:17:00.000
Then we can right-click

361
00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:04.380
and promote the fade texture to parameter.

362
00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:09.140
And we can set its slider mean to something like minus one

363
00:17:09.140 --> 00:17:12.210
and the max to 0.5.

364
00:17:12.210 --> 00:17:15.410
Okay, then let's right-click on the dissolve effect

365
00:17:15.410 --> 00:17:17.769
and promote two parameter

366
00:17:17.769 --> 00:17:21.369
and we can set the slider max to one.

367
00:17:21.369 --> 00:17:23.970
Lastly, let's right-click on interior tint

368
00:17:23.970 --> 00:17:27.039
and promote that to parameter as well.

369
00:17:27.039 --> 00:17:33.829
And let's give it a tint that we can see like pink.

370
00:17:33.829 --> 00:17:36.710
Now we can go ahead and plug the output pin

371
00:17:36.710 --> 00:17:39.589
of the opacity mask into the input bit

372
00:17:39.589 --> 00:17:42.430
of the opacity mask of our main material.

373
00:17:42.430 --> 00:17:45.109
And then we want to be able to control

374
00:17:45.109 --> 00:17:47.430
the strength of our emissive.

375
00:17:47.430 --> 00:17:49.509
So let's press S on our keyboard

376
00:17:49.509 --> 00:17:51.349
to create a scalar parameter

377
00:17:51.349 --> 00:17:54.349
that we can call emissive strength.

378
00:17:54.349 --> 00:17:57.869
Now we want to press M to multiply it.

379
00:17:57.869 --> 00:18:00.589
So let's connect the emissive output

380
00:18:00.589 --> 00:18:03.230
of the MF teleporter to the A

381
00:18:03.230 --> 00:18:05.349
and the emissive strength to the B.

382
00:18:05.349 --> 00:18:07.750
And then let's connect the output pin of the multiplier

383
00:18:07.750 --> 00:18:09.710
to the emissive color.

384
00:18:09.710 --> 00:18:15.130
Okay, we can save and then navigate to our viewport.

385
00:18:15.490 --> 00:18:18.809
And in here, we can right-click on our parent material

386
00:18:18.809 --> 00:18:21.690
and then create a material instance for it.

387
00:18:21.690 --> 00:18:25.130
So I'm going to be calling it MI and the score dissolve

388
00:18:26.849 --> 00:18:30.589
and control shift dash apply all.

389
00:18:30.589 --> 00:18:33.990
Okay, now if I drag this material instance

390
00:18:33.990 --> 00:18:36.750
onto one of the cans,

391
00:18:36.750 --> 00:18:38.950
it's going to be applied directly.

392
00:18:38.950 --> 00:18:43.769
And let's double-click on it to have it here undocked.

393
00:18:43.769 --> 00:18:46.609
I'm going to be playing around with the dissolve offset,

394
00:18:46.609 --> 00:18:50.250
the emissive strength, the fade texture, and the length.

395
00:18:50.250 --> 00:18:52.769
Let's start by playing a bit with the length.

396
00:18:52.809 --> 00:18:56.849
You can see here that our material will start dissolving

397
00:18:56.849 --> 00:18:59.210
and right now the fade texture is set to zero

398
00:18:59.210 --> 00:19:02.589
so we don't see any texture at all.

399
00:19:02.589 --> 00:19:04.789
And if we increase the emissive strength,

400
00:19:04.789 --> 00:19:08.990
also our interior tint will start shining through.

401
00:19:08.990 --> 00:19:10.309
And here with the dissolve offset,

402
00:19:10.309 --> 00:19:11.950
you can see that we can really define

403
00:19:11.950 --> 00:19:15.430
where this cut is happening on the can.

404
00:19:15.430 --> 00:19:18.259
Let's switch back to our slides now.

405
00:19:18.259 --> 00:19:20.539
So that was it for this class,

406
00:19:20.539 --> 00:19:23.779
but you can find some additional documentation at this link

407
00:19:24.339 --> 00:19:26.420
if you're interested in further expanding your knowledge

408
00:19:26.420 --> 00:19:28.299
in material functions.

409
00:19:28.299 --> 00:19:31.019
And here you can also find even more resources

410
00:19:31.019 --> 00:19:33.460
to dive deeper into animated materials

411
00:19:33.460 --> 00:19:35.900
and customize them to your liking.

412
00:19:35.900 --> 00:19:38.740
We're almost at the end of this entire series of classes

413
00:19:38.740 --> 00:19:41.259
on translucent materials and VFX.

414
00:19:41.259 --> 00:19:43.619
We won't be diving into hands-on exercises

415
00:19:43.619 --> 00:19:45.460
or new theories any further,

416
00:19:45.460 --> 00:19:47.740
but in the upcoming lessons, we will do a recap

417
00:19:47.740 --> 00:19:51.339
on everything we learned so far and say our goodbyes.

418
00:19:51.339 --> 00:19:54.380
Thank you for sticking around and I'll see you there.

419
00:19:54.380 --> 00:19:55.220
Bye.
